.\nAccordingly, we now refer to ρ as the conjugate moment measure of w, w as the conjugate moment\npotential of ρ, and Pw as the conjugate Gibbs factor of ρ. Our proof strategy differs significantly\nfrom that used in Cordero-Erausquin and Klartag [2015] and Santambrogio [2016] since we use\nSchauder’s fixed point theorem to show that the following map admits a fixed point:\nGρ : L(Rd) →L(Rd) ,\nGρ(w) := B(ρ, Pw)\n(7)\nwhere L(Rd) is the set of functions mapping Rd to R ∪{+∞}, and Gρ assigns to a potential w the\nBrenier potential transporting ρ to Pw. Fixed points of Gρ correspond precisely to the conjugate\nmoment potentials of ρ, and their existence guarantees a solution to the moment measure factorization.\nA sketch of the proof is provided in Appendix A.1, with full details in Appendix A.2. When ρ is\nGaussian, the conjugate moment measure factorization admits an explicit solution, as given in\nProposition 2. Recall that we saw in §3.1 that Gibbs factors of N(0, Σ) were multivariate normal\ndistributions with covariance matrix Σ−1.\nProposition 2. When Σ is non-degenerate, ρ = N(m, Σ) is the conjugate moment measure of\nw(x) =\n1\n2(x −r)T Σ−1/3(x −r), whose Gibbs distribution is Pw = N(r, Σ1/3) where r =\n(Id + Σ1/3)−1m. The function w is the unique conjugate moment potential of ρ whose Gibbs\ndistribution remains Gaussian.\nThe proof is provided in Appendix A.4 and relies on the fact that the OT map between two Gaussians\nis known in closed form [Peyré et al., 2019]. While this potential w may not be the only conjugate\nmoment potential, the Gibbs distribution associated with it, N(r, Σ1/3), appears to be better suited to\nthe target distribution ρ. This is particularly evident in the example shown in Figure 1. Additionally,\nit is worth noting that for the Gaussian case, our factorization still works when ρ is not centered,\nwhereas the approach of Cordero-Erausquin and Klartag [2015] requires ρ to be mean 0.\n3.3\nMonge-Ampère Equation for Conjugate Moment 